# ops.fayth — the Ops persona: receives production events, matches an SOP, executes or
# escalates. It is the only persona whose work arrives from outside the plan.
#
# A fayth is a persona DEFINITION; an aeon is one summoned instance of it. Four things:
# a predicate carving its partition out of the graph, the memories it must read, the tools
# it may use, and what wakes it.

FAYTH_NAME=ops
FAYTH_DESC="Ops — the healer: claims incidents and sweeps, matches an SOP, executes it or writes one"

# The partition. Incidents are filed by incident.sh from a systemd OnFailure, never by
# hand and never by the plan, so this can only ever see production events. The escalation
# label is excluded for the same reason it is everywhere: an escalation must never be dispatched as
# if it were work.
FAYTH_LABELS="spira,incident"
FAYTH_EXCLUDE_LABELS="spira-poison,$SPIRA_ASK_LABEL,$SPIRA_CI_LABEL"

# BOTH BOOKS. Statutes are how to behave; SOPs are how to fix — and Ops is the only
# persona that needs the second. The builder reads `law-` alone, which is what keeps a
# growing shelf of runbooks out of the context of every agent that will never execute one.
FAYTH_MEMORY_PREFIXES="law-,sop-"

# NO FAYTH_REPO. The repository comes from the BEAD — a `repo:<name>` label resolved
# through .claude/spira/repo-map — because a repository pinned per persona is a
# persona that can only ever work one of them, and every fayth here named the same
# one. A bead with no `repo:` label is the home repo, which is what Spira's own plan
# beads are.
FAYTH_MODEL=claude-opus-5
FAYTH_MAX_CONCURRENT=1

# A PARTY MEMBER, NOT AN AEON. Ops is Yuna: it travels with the party and is always around.
# A builder is Ifrit — called for one fight and dismissed after it. So Ops is NOT drawn from
# SPIRA_MAX_AEONS and cannot be crowded out of it: it has its own summoner
# (spira-ops.timer), and watchtower.sh gives it the pipeline's vital signs on a timer so the
# role has something to be persistent about rather than existing only on paper.
#
# The first version of this gave Ops a reserved slot INSIDE the aeon pool. That was the
# wrong shape for the same reason: a reserved slot is still a slot somebody must release,
# and builders hold theirs for 45-90 minutes. A role that never competes cannot be starved.
FAYTH_ROLE=party

# BOUNDED BY THE SWEEP INTERVAL, which is the number that actually constrains this persona.
# watchtower.sh files a health sweep every SPIRA_WATCH_EVERY (10 minutes), so an Ops session
# that can run for an hour — the default TIMEOUT_SECONDS is 3600 — would still be working the
# 22:30 snapshot while the 22:40, 22:50 and 23:00 ones arrived behind it. Observed at six
# minutes and climbing on the first live sweep. Sessions must not outlive the cadence that
# produces their work.
#
# EIGHT MINUTES, HARD. systemd kills the unit at TIMEOUT_SECONDS, so this is a wall rather
# than a request. Two minutes of headroom under the ten, because a sweep that overruns by
# thirty seconds still collides.
#
# THE BOUND IS A FEATURE, not a compromise. An Ops aeon that cannot finish must write what it
# found into the graph as beads and let the next sweep continue — which is what we want it
# doing anyway. Findings held in a session that gets killed are lost; findings cut into beads
# survive and are what the next aeon starts from. The lease matches, so a killed session's
# bead is reclaimable promptly rather than sitting held for another 37 minutes.
FAYTH_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=480
FAYTH_LEASE_MINUTES=10
FAYTH_HEARTBEAT_SECONDS=60
FAYTH_STALL_BEATS=6

FAYTH_TOOLS="Bash,Read,Edit,Write,Glob,Grep,TodoWrite"
